# The oracle references, pinned.
#
# One line per reference, tab-separated: <name> <image> <version> <what it
# answers>.
#
# **Every line names the same image**, which is not tidiness. These are five
# independent implementations asked the same question about the same file,
# and the whole value is in where they disagree. In one image per reference,
# with one base state each, a disagreement would first have to be shown not
# to be the images' fault. One image makes "the reference set" one fact.
#
# The image is built here, so the tag is not a digest of the finished image:
# two builds of one Containerfile are not two copies of one image. What is
# pinned instead is everything the build reads - the base by digest, the
# packages by full apt version, resolved against a dated snapshot of the
# Debian archive so the pins do not expire - and the run-time version check
# is the real guarantee. oracle_env.check_pin() therefore runs in *both*
# modes; there is no stock image named here, so relaxing it in host mode
# would leave nothing checking anything.
#
# Three of them need a word about what the number is:
#
#   - For libsndfile it is the **C library's** version and not
#     python-soundfile's. soundfile is a ctypes wrapper; libsndfile is what
#     decides an answer, and the two move independently.
#   - mutagen was added in phase 3, and the reason is worth keeping: ffmpeg
#     is what *generates* the tag fixtures, so scoring them with ffmpeg
#     alone would be asking one implementation whether it agrees with
#     itself. A misunderstanding held in both its writer and its reader is
#     invisible to that. mutagen shares no code with it.
#   - For flac it is the reference implementation's own version, and it is
#     the one pin here that covers three of the lines below. sox reaches
#     FLAC through libsox_fmt_flac, libsndfile links libFLAC, and `flac`
#     is libFLAC's front end - so **for FLAC there are two implementations
#     in this image, not five**: ffmpeg's native decoder, and libFLAC
#     answering three times. libflac14 is pinned in the Containerfile for
#     that reason. Do not read "three references agree about a FLAC file"
#     as corroboration; check_corpus.py groups them.
#   - For pywave it is the interpreter version, because `wave` is a stdlib
#     module with no version of its own and its behaviour has changed
#     between releases. Note what that same release took away: **Python 3.13
#     removed `aifc`** under PEP 594, so there is no stdlib AIFF reader any
#     more. AIFF is scored by ffmpeg, sox and libsndfile; WAV by all four.
#
# Raising a pin is deliberate. A new reference version is new answers, and a
# new answer that disagrees is a finding to triage rather than a regression
# to fix.
#
# Building it:
#   make oracle-build

ffmpeg	localhost/ghoti-audio-oracle-refs:deb13-3	ffmpeg 7.1.5	libavformat's RIFF and IFF demuxers; the most widely deployed reader of both formats
sox	localhost/ghoti-audio-oracle-refs:deb13-3	SoX 14.4.2	A separate lineage from ffmpeg, and the reference happiest being asked for a raw sample dump
libsndfile	localhost/ghoti-audio-oracle-refs:deb13-3	libsndfile 1.2.2	The third parser, and the one most non-ffmpeg audio applications actually use
pywave	localhost/ghoti-audio-oracle-refs:deb13-3	Python 3.13.5 wave	A pure-Python fourth reading that shares no code with the three C parsers. WAV only; 3.13 removed aifc
mutagen	localhost/ghoti-audio-oracle-refs:deb13-3	mutagen 1.47.0	The only reference here that exists for metadata, and the implementation most taggers are built on. Tags only; it reads no samples
flac	localhost/ghoti-audio-oracle-refs:deb13-3	flac 1.5.0	The format's own reference implementation. `flac -t` verifies a file's STREAMINFO MD5, which scores the whole decoder at once; libFLAC underneath is also what sox and libsndfile answer FLAC questions with
opusdec	localhost/ghoti-audio-oracle-refs:deb13-3	opusdec 1.5.2	libopus through opus-tools' front end - the implementation RFC 6716 defines conformance against. The *libopus* version is pinned and not opus-tools', because libopus is what decides an answer
opus_compare	localhost/ghoti-audio-oracle-refs:deb13-3	opus_compare 1.5.2	**The only normative reference in this image.** RFC 6716 defines a conforming decoder as one whose output this tool accepts, so it answers pass or fail rather than a number to argue about. Built from the libopus source of the same snapshot, because Debian packages the library and not this one file
mpg123	localhost/ghoti-audio-oracle-refs:deb13-3	mpg123 1.32.10	libmpg123 through its front end: the decoder most MP3 players that are not ffmpeg are built on, and the one whose gapless rule (the LAME extension, read only when its name says so) is its own code. **One implementation with libsndfile**, which reaches MP3 through libmpg123

# Not here yet:
#   afconvert  Apple's own, and the reference that decides what AIFF-C really
#              means. It is macOS-only, so it cannot live in this image; when
#              something turns on a disagreement about AIFF-C it will have to
#              be run by hand and its answer committed.
# Raised to deb13-2 by phase 6, which added the two Opus lines above, and to
# deb13-3 when the MP3 encoder came and mpg123 was added to score it.
# §11.13's rule applies to them as it does to FLAC's four: **for Opus the
# count is two, and the second is inside ffmpeg** - libavcodec has a
# native Opus decoder as well as a libopus wrapper, and `-c:a opus`
# selects the native one. opusdec and ffmpeg are therefore two
# implementations in the way phase 5's two MPEG decoders were.
#
# Phase 6's own trap was a different shape and is about Vorbis: ffmpeg's
# two Vorbis decoders are two implementations for sample *values* and one
# reading for the *length*, because the trim happens in the demuxer they
# share. And libsndfile *wraps* on an int16 overflow where ffmpeg clips,
# so the two swap round between the two questions. planning/audio.md
# §§11.25 and 11.29.
#
# Not here, and deliberately:
#   oggdec     vorbis-tools' decoder, which is libvorbis - the same
#              implementation libsndfile already answers Vorbis questions
#              with. A third name for a second implementation is what
#              §11.13 is about; it would add a row and no information.
